u.email) = 'a@example.org';`\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users` (non-sargable predicate).** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The planner has to read every user row and lowercase each email. This is the main cost.\n2. **Scan of `orders` on `user_id`.** If `orders.user_id` has no index, the join becomes a full scan of `orders`, either as a hash join or as a nested loop that rescans `orders` for each matching user. If the FK column is indexed, this is a cheap index lookup. Many schemas don't index FK columns automatically, so check.\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, including any wide text or JSON columns. It also prevents index-only scans. The result has duplicate column names (`id`, `created_at`, and so on), which can break clients that map columns by name.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**1. Index the expression (PostgreSQL):**\n```sql\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n```\nThe query can then do an index seek instead of scanning `users`. Make it unique if emails are meant to be case-insensitively unique. That also enforces the rule and tells the planner at most one user matches.\n\nIn MySQL 8, use a functional index: `CREATE INDEX ... ON users ((lower(email)));`. In SQL Server, use a computed column plus an index on it. Or use a case-insensitive collation or `citext` type and drop the `lower()` call entirely.\n\n**2. Index the join key:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\nThis makes the join a lookup of one user's orders. If you usually sort by recency, `(user_id, created_at DESC)` serves that too.\n\n**3. Rewrite to select only what you need:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.status, o.total, o.created_at, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nListing columns cuts I/O and avoids the name collisions. Put `users` first to show the intended order: one user by key, then their orders. The planner will normally pick this order anyway.\n\n**Alternative:** if you can normalize on write (store `email` already lowercased and lowercase the input in the app), use `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'` with an ordinary unique index on `email`. This is the simplest option and needs no expression index.\n\n## Caveats\n- I only have the query text, not the schema, row counts or existing indexes. Confirm with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after. In particular, check whether the `orders.user_id` index already exists.\n- If one user can have thousands of orders, add a `LIMIT` or pagination to avoid returning them all.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}